### 📂 4. The Bootstrapper (Dummy Data Runner)

This class runs automatically when the application starts.

```java
@Component
public class DataInitializer implements CommandLineRunner {

    @Autowired
    private GadgetService gadgetService;

    @Autowired
    private GadgetRepository gadgetRepository;

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) throws Exception {
        System.out.println("--- Starting Data Insertion (Joined Strategy) ---");

        // Save a Laptop
        gadgetService.saveGadget("LAPTOP", "Apple", 150000, 16, "M3 Pro", false);

        // Save a Smartphone
        gadgetService.saveGadget("PHONE", "Samsung", 85000, 5000, null, true);

        System.out.println("--- Data Saved! Fetching all gadgets from DB ---");

        // Fetching all to prove Polymorphism works
        gadgetRepository.findAll().forEach(g -> {
            System.out.println("Found Gadget: " + g.getBrand() + " | Price: " + g.getPrice());
            
            if (g instanceof Laptop) {
                System.out.println("-> Type: Laptop | CPU: " + ((Laptop) g).getProcessor());
            } else if (g instanceof Smartphone) {
                System.out.println("-> Type: Phone | Battery: " + ((Smartphone) g).getBatteryCapacity());
            }
        });
    }
}

```

---

### 🧠 Critical Observations (The "Senior" View)

1. **Check the Console Logs:** You will see Hibernate generate two `INSERT` statements for every save. One for `gadget` and one for the specific child table.
2. **No NULLs:** Open your Database. You will notice that the `laptop` table **only** has columns for RAM and Processor. It does not have battery columns. This is the main benefit of **Joined**.
3. **The Common Link:** All tables share the same `id`. The `id` in the `laptop` table is both a Primary Key and a Foreign Key pointing to the `id` in the `gadget` table.

